# .spool/server/server.req

feature: SERVER

requirements:

  - id: SERVER-1
    title: Stdio Transport
    description: >
      The MCP server must communicate with clients over stdio
      using the JSON-RPC framing defined by the Model Context
      Protocol specification.
    status: active
    deciders:
      - Matt Ames
    consulted: []
    date: 2026-05-04
    rationale: >
      Stdio is the canonical MCP transport for locally launched
      servers and is the only transport coding-agent clients are
      guaranteed to support. Picking a single transport keeps
      the server free of network and lifecycle concerns it does
      not need.
    superseded_by:
    acceptance_criteria:

      - id: SERVER-1-1
        title: Reads Requests from Stdin
        description: >
          Given a running server,
          when a JSON-RPC request is written to its stdin,
          then the server parses the request and dispatches
          it to the appropriate handler.

      - id: SERVER-1-2
        title: Writes Responses to Stdout
        description: >
          Given a request that has been dispatched,
          when the handler produces a response,
          then the response is written to stdout in the
          JSON-RPC framing required by MCP.

      - id: SERVER-1-3
        title: Logs Go to Stderr
        description: >
          Given the server is running,
          when it emits log or diagnostic output,
          then that output is written to stderr so it does
          not corrupt the JSON-RPC stream on stdout.

  - id: SERVER-2
    title: Tool Advertisement
    description: >
      The MCP server must advertise the full set of v1 tools in
      response to the MCP tools/list request, with each tool
      exposing the input schema clients need to call it.
    status: active
    deciders:
      - Matt Ames
    consulted: []
    date: 2026-05-04
    rationale: >
      Clients discover available tools via tools/list and rely
      on the advertised input schemas to construct valid calls.
      Locking the v1 tool set into a single requirement makes
      the server's contract explicit and makes regressions
      (a missing or renamed tool) easy to detect.
    superseded_by:
    acceptance_criteria:

      - id: SERVER-2-1
        title: All v1 Tools Listed
        description: >
          Given a running server,
          when the client issues tools/list,
          then the response includes get_coverage_report,
          list_untested_acs, list_orphaned_annotations,
          list_validation_errors, get_requirement,
          get_acceptance_criterion, list_requirements,
          and list_features.

      - id: SERVER-2-2
        title: Each Tool Declares Input Schema
        description: >
          Given a running server,
          when the client issues tools/list,
          then every advertised tool includes a JSON Schema
          describing its accepted arguments, including the
          required project argument.

      - id: SERVER-2-3
        title: Each Tool Declares a Description
        description: >
          Given a running server,
          when the client issues tools/list,
          then every advertised tool includes a human-readable
          description sufficient for an agent to choose between
          tools without reading external documentation.

  - id: SERVER-3
    title: Tool Error Response Shape
    description: >
      When a tool handler fails, the server must return a
      structured error response identifying the failure category
      so that clients can react appropriately rather than
      surfacing an opaque string.
    status: active
    deciders:
      - Matt Ames
    consulted: []
    date: 2026-05-04
    rationale: >
      Coding agents are the primary clients of this server and
      they recover from failures more reliably when error
      responses carry a category they can branch on. The
      categories chosen here mirror the failure modes a Spool
      project can present: the project argument is bad, the
      .spool.yaml is missing or malformed, the .req files do not
      parse, or the requested ID does not exist.
    superseded_by:
    acceptance_criteria:

      - id: SERVER-3-1
        title: Invalid Project Path
        description: >
          Given a tool call whose project argument does not
          resolve to an existing directory,
          when the tool is invoked,
          then the response is an error with category
          "invalid_project" and a message identifying the path.

      - id: SERVER-3-2
        title: Missing Spool Config
        description: >
          Given a tool call whose project resolves to a directory
          that does not contain a .spool.yaml,
          when the tool is invoked,
          then the response is an error with category
          "missing_config" and a message identifying the path.

      - id: SERVER-3-3
        title: Unknown ID
        description: >
          Given a lookup tool call referencing a Spool ID that
          does not exist in the project,
          when the tool is invoked,
          then the response is a successful result containing
          a null value, not an error.

      - id: SERVER-3-4
        title: Unexpected Internal Failure
        description: >
          Given a tool call that triggers an unexpected internal
          error,
          when the tool is invoked,
          then the response is an error with category "internal"
          and a message describing the failure, and the server
          continues running and accepting subsequent requests.
